A||N||5||RDosdorian||V-8 Fieros (California Smog)||||8||
Z||000000||RDosdorian||05-25-2000||04:13 PM||||OK, I had Archie build a V-8 for me.  It's an LT1 conversion and oh my god it's fast.  However, the state of California is giving me problems.  It passes the actual smog test on the dyno but it will not pass visual.  The smog ref tells me it has the wrong wiring harness, wrong 02 sensors, needs AIR system, blah, blah, blah.  You get the idea (I hope) For those of you in California, how did you get through the California BS.  Any help would be appreciated||||reg||8
Z||000001||Cooter||05-25-2000||04:55 PM||||I am not from the west coast, so correct me if I am wrong but:<BR>The if fiero never had an AIR system, if the LT1 never had an AIR system, so why would the 2 together have an AIR system?<P>It sounds like your smog guy is just looking for trouble- wrong wiring and o2 sensors? The wiring must match the donor engine, so if it is running you MUST have the right harness. Wait until that guy is gone and try again maybe?||||reg||1
Z||000002||BN Boomer||05-25-2000||05:10 PM||||Hey, that inspectors name wouldn't happen to be M.... M..... Mi...... Oh geez, I just can't say it!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I'd try going to a different inspection station, maybe one out in the middle of the Mohave desert where they probably aren't so anal about such things.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000003||stimpy||05-25-2000||07:41 PM||||Problem with CA smog checks are the car must pass check in the area it is registered in. I bought my Fiero in Stockton which has Smog II requirements (dyno test) and was unable to have it tested 20 miles away in Tracy, a small town not budened by Smog II requirements. I will email this page to my dad (Big O)and see if he can shoot you any words of wisdom he gained by getting his S/C 3.8 past the referee.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Big O||05-25-2000||10:52 PM||||Getting the 3.8 past smog was a relatively simple procedure after I finally got all of rhe cr#p fixed that my friends at American Custom Engineering did to it when they installed it.  I took it to the smog referee in Fremont. I know Stimpy said you had to have the car inspected in the county where you live, but somehow I got special dispensation from Sacramento to have mine done in Fremont even though I lived in Tracy at the time.  I did have to meet Alameda county specs, but that wasn't too tough.  The referee was very fair.  He inspected the car to see that everything that was supposed to be there was in fact there and working.  It had to meet 97 Bonnevile specs as that's where the engine came from.  The powerplant was bone stock except for the exhaust system,  which I thought would cause me the most problems.  My system has no muffler, only a low restriction cat and ANSA tips.  The referee didn't even bat an eye about that.  He stuck the tube up the pipes, ran the test and commented that the engine was so clean you could almost sniff directly from the pipe with your nose.  (This was probably because my ACE exhaust system was about to fall off the car). He installed a sticker on the doorsill and took my money and sent me on my way.  I don't know if you've had the engine modified, but if you haven't it may be a situation where you may need to educate the referee on the configuration of your car.  Documentation is important.  You've got to be able to prove where the engine came from and what "stock" configuration really means.  Remember your dealing with a government agency run by semi-literate, low-paid workers, some who are on power trips.  Be patient and perserverant.||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||05-25-2000||11:46 PM||||Your statement is worded to imply the wiring harness is not smog legal. I've been through this (like a few others here) and I think he means that you must use all of the same emissions equipment that came with the car and wire it accordingly. Same O2's, same cats, same pumps, etc. <P>And therein lies a problem. The V8's with manual trans usually had an air-pump. If so, then you have to add it in. That's what makes the automatics so nice in Califonia. At the same time, you should be able to add it without much hassle. <P>If you do it the way they want, they will bless it. Otherwise they will show you a really big stick. <P>Did you get the EXACT details on what they wanted changed? I've found that they actually like engine swaps and are usually very helpful. You might also try a different referee.||||reg||1
